<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as French National Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Convention was the elected assembly that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from September 1792 to October 1795. On its first full day it abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the French Republic, and in 1793 it tried and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Under pressure of war and revolt it gave power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, which ran the Terror, and after Robespierre's fall it drafted the constitution of the Directory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-convention.jpg" alt="National Convention" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fluffy89502 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_National_Convention_Composition_Pre-purge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Delegates at the <b>National Convention</b> of 1908–1909, among them the Cape prime minister John X. Merriman, agreed a constitution that kept the Cape’s non-racial franchise within the province but allowed only white men to sit in the Union parliament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>War with Austria from April 1792 and fear of betrayal led to the storming of the Tuileries on 10 August 1792; the <b>National Convention</b> abolished the monarchy on 21 September 1792, and Louis XVI was guillotined in January 1793.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 October 1795 the <b>National Convention</b> annexed the principality to France, dividing it chiefly between the departments of Ourthe and Meuse-Inférieure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National Convention</b>'s decree of 15 May 1792 restored the rights of the free people of colour; Léger-Félicité Sonthonax, Civil Commissioner and de facto ruler from September 1792 to 1795, stripped the white Creoles of military power and abolished slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivalry among the four colonies over railways and customs duties, together with a wish among white politicians to settle ‘native policy’ on a common footing, led to the <b>National Convention</b> of 1908–1909, which met in Durban, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>French commissioners freed them in 1793, and on 4 February 1794 the <b>National Convention</b> abolished slavery in all French colonies.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
